from .lg import S
PROBLEMS = [
 (60, ("(GATE/CSIR style) Arrange in order of <b>increasing</b> pK<sub>a</sub> in <b>water</b> (i.e. decreasing C–H acidity): acetone, ethyl acetate, diethyl malonate, nitromethane, acetylacetone.",
   None,
   "acetylacetone (9) &lt; nitromethane (10) &lt; diethyl malonate (13) &lt; acetone (19) &lt; ethyl acetate (≈25)",
   "Two carbonyl groups (acetylacetone, malonate) share the charge over two oxygens, and a ketone stabilises the carbanion better than an ester because the ester carbonyl is already donated into by OEt. Nitromethane (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 10) is the most acidic mono-activated C–H acid because NO<sub>2</sub> is the strongest acceptor. ⚑ The order is solvent-dependent: in DMSO the values are 13.3 (acac), 16.4 (malonate), 17.2 (MeNO<sub>2</sub>), 26.5 (acetone), 29.5 (EtOAc), so malonate and nitromethane swap. In water the nitronate oxygens are strongly H-bonded, which stabilises it more than the delocalised malonate enolate, whereas in DMSO that solvation advantage disappears.")),
 (61, ("(JEE Advanced style) 2-Methylcyclohexanone is treated (a) with LDA (1.05 equiv), THF, −78 °C, then PhCH<sub>2</sub>Br; (b) with KH (0.9 equiv), THF, 25 °C, 2 h, then PhCH<sub>2</sub>Br. Give the major product of each and name the type of enolate involved.",
   S([("2-methylcyclohexanone", "CC1CCCCC1=O"), ("(a)", "O=C1C(Cc2ccccc2)CCCC1C"), ("(b)", "O=C1C(C)(Cc2ccccc2)CCCC1")]),
   "(a) 2-benzyl-6-methylcyclohexanone (kinetic enolate); (b) 2-benzyl-2-methylcyclohexanone (thermodynamic enolate)",
   "LDA is bulky, strong and used in slight excess at low temperature, so deprotonation is fast and irreversible and occurs at the less hindered C-6 CH<sub>2</sub>, giving the less substituted (kinetic) enolate, which is alkylated at C-6. With a sub-stoichiometric strong base at room temperature, proton transfer between enolate and ketone equilibrates the enolates; the more substituted (tetrasubstituted) enolate toward C-2 is more stable and gives the 2,2-disubstituted product.")),
 (62, ("(Assertion-Reason) <b>Assertion:</b> 2-Oxocyclohexanecarboxylic acid loses CO<sub>2</sub> readily on gentle warming, but the bicyclic acid shown (a bridgehead carboxylic acid β to a ketone) is stable to heating at 150 °C. <b>Reason:</b> Decarboxylation of a β-keto acid proceeds through a six-membered cyclic transition state that produces an enol, and for the bicyclic acid this enol would need a C=C at a bridgehead of a small bicycle (Bredt violation).<br>(A) Both true, Reason explains Assertion; (B) both true, Reason does not explain; (C) Assertion true, Reason false; (D) Assertion false, Reason true.",
   S([("2-oxocyclohexane-COOH", "O=C1CCCCC1C(=O)O"), ("bicyclic keto acid", "O=C1CC2CCC1(C2)C(=O)O")]),
   "(A)",
   "β-Keto acids decarboxylate through a concerted six-membered pericyclic TS (O=C–C–C=O with the acid O–H transferring to the ketone O); the immediate product is an enol that tautomerises to the ketone. In the norbornanone acid the carbon bearing COOH is a bridgehead, so the enol C=C would have to be placed at the bridgehead of a bicyclo[2.2.1] system, which is impossible (Bredt), so the acid survives. Thus the Reason explains the Assertion: (A).")),
 (63, ("(JEE Advanced, match the column) Match each named reaction in Column I with the product of the typical example in Column II (structures 1-4 in the figure). P: Reformatsky (PhCHO, BrCH<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Et, Zn, then H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>); Q: Darzens (PhCHO, ClCH<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Et, NaOEt); R: Knoevenagel (PhCHO, CH<sub>2</sub>(CO<sub>2</sub>Et)<sub>2</sub>, piperidine, heat); S: Dieckmann (EtO<sub>2</sub>C(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>4</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Et, NaOEt, then H<sub>3</sub>O<sup>+</sup>).",
   S([("1", "CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O"), ("2", "CCOC(=O)C1OC1c1ccccc1"), ("3", "CCOC(=O)CC(O)c1ccccc1"), ("4", "CCOC(=O)C(=Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)OCC")]),
   "P–3, Q–2, R–4, S–1",
   "Reformatsky: the organozinc enolate (too unreactive to attack the ester) adds to the aldehyde to give the β-hydroxy ester 3. Darzens: the α-chloro ester enolate adds to PhCHO and the resulting alkoxide displaces chloride (3-exo-tet) to give the glycidic ester 2. Knoevenagel: the malonate carbanion adds, then dehydration (E1cB) gives benzylidene malonate 4. Dieckmann: intramolecular Claisen of diethyl adipate gives the five-membered β-keto ester 1.")),
 (64, ("(Olympiad style) Two ylides are generated: Ph<sub>3</sub>P=CHCO<sub>2</sub>Et (stable, isolable) and Ph<sub>3</sub>P=CHCH<sub>3</sub> (from the phosphonium salt and NaNH<sub>2</sub> or KHMDS, salt-free THF). Each reacts with benzaldehyde. Give the major organic product of each, including alkene geometry, and the common by-product. Why do the geometries differ?",
   None,
   "(E)-ethyl cinnamate and (Z)-1-phenylpropene; Ph<sub>3</sub>P=O",
   "Non-stabilised ylides add under kinetic control through an early, puckered TS to give the cis-oxaphosphetane irreversibly, which collapses (syn-elimination) to the Z-alkene. The ester-stabilised ylide is less reactive and its additions are reversible, so the thermodynamically more stable trans-oxaphosphetane is funnelled to the E-alkene. The driving force in both is the very strong P=O bond of Ph<sub>3</sub>P=O. ⚑ Lithium salts can erode Z-selectivity for non-stabilised ylides (hence the salt-free conditions).")),
 (65, ("(JEE Advanced, integer) Excess MeMgBr is used and the <b>minimum</b> number of moles of MeMgBr consumed per mole of substrate is counted (Grignard acts as a base towards O–H and C≡C–H, and does not deprotonate the ester α-CH). Add the numbers for: (i) ethyl 4-hydroxybenzoate; (ii) ethyl 4-ethynylbenzoate; (iii) diethyl carbonate. Report the sum.",
   None, "9",
   "(i) 1 equiv deprotonates the phenol (ArOMgBr), then 2 equiv add to the ester (ketone intermediate is more reactive than the ester), giving after work-up 4-(2-hydroxypropan-2-yl)phenol: 3. (ii) The terminal alkyne C–H (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 25) is deprotonated (1) and the ester gives the tertiary alcohol (2): 3. (iii) Diethyl carbonate takes three Me groups, giving (CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>3</sub>COH after work-up: 3. Sum = 9.")),
 (66, ("(Olympiad style) The lithium enolate of 2,2-dimethylpentan-3-one (t-Bu ethyl ketone) formed with LDA in THF is the (Z)-enolate, in which OLi and the CH<sub>3</sub> group of the enolate C=C are on the same side. It reacts with benzaldehyde at −78 °C. (a) Which chair transition state geometry (Zimmerman–Traxler) results and where do the t-Bu and Ph groups sit? (b) Is the aldol syn or anti? (c) How many stereoisomers form, and what would an (E)-enolate give?",
   S([("Z-enolate", r"C/C=C(\O[Li])C(C)(C)C"), ("PhCHO", "O=Cc1ccccc1"), ("aldol (constitution)", "CC(C(O)c1ccccc1)C(=O)C(C)(C)C")]),
   "(b) syn; (c) one racemic diastereomer (2 enantiomers); E-enolate gives the anti aldol",
   "The enolate O–Li–O=C(H)Ph forms a six-membered chair: Li bridges the enolate O and the aldehyde O. The aldehyde Ph prefers the pseudo-equatorial position to avoid 1,3-diaxial interactions with the ligands on Li and the enolate substituents. With a Z-enolate the enolate methyl is forced pseudo-axial next to the aldehyde H, and this chair delivers the syn aldol (Me and OH on the same side in the zig-zag chain). The E-enolate places Me equatorial and gives the anti aldol. Each diastereomer is racemic since no chiral element is present. ⚑ syn/anti refers to the Masamune zig-zag convention.")),
 (67, ("(IChO-style) Diethyl hexanedioate (diethyl adipate) is treated with (i) NaOEt (1 equiv), EtOH, reflux; (ii) NaOEt, then MeI; (iii) aq. HCl, heat. Give the compounds A, B and the final ketone C, and say why a full equivalent of alkoxide is needed in step (i) and why the same sequence from diethyl pentanedioate (glutarate) fails.",
   S([("diethyl adipate", "CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC"), ("A", "CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O"), ("B", "CCOC(=O)C1(C)CCCC1=O"), ("C", "CC1CCCC1=O")]),
   "A: ethyl 2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate; B: its 1-methyl derivative; C: 2-methylcyclopentanone",
   "Dieckmann cyclisation: the ester enolate attacks the other ester to form a five-membered ring, expelling EtO<sup>−</sup>. The β-keto ester product (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 12) is deprotonated by ethoxide, and this irreversible step drives the otherwise unfavourable equilibrium, so one full equivalent of base is consumed. The stabilised enolate is C-alkylated by MeI to B (quaternary, no acidic H). Acid hydrolysis gives the β-keto acid which decarboxylates (cyclic six-membered TS) to C. Glutarate would have to close a strained four-membered ring and does not cyclise.")),
 (68, ("(JEE Advanced, integer) Use pK<sub>a</sub>(EtOH) = 16, pK<sub>a</sub>(CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Et, C–H) = 25 and pK<sub>a</sub>(ethyl acetoacetate, C-2 H) = 11. (i) Let K<sub>1</sub> be the equilibrium constant for CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Et + EtO<sup>−</sup> ⇌ enolate + EtOH, and p = −log<sub>10</sub>K<sub>1</sub>. (ii) The neutral Claisen condensation 2 CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Et ⇌ CH<sub>3</sub>COCH<sub>2</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Et + EtOH is assumed to have K = 1×10<sup>−3</sup> (for the purposes of this problem). Let K<sub>2</sub> be the constant for 2 CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>Et + EtO<sup>−</sup> ⇌ acetoacetate enolate + 2 EtOH. Give p and log<sub>10</sub>K<sub>2</sub>.",
   None, "p = 9; log K<sub>2</sub> = +2",
   "K<sub>1</sub> = 10<sup>pKa(EtOH) − pKa(ester)</sup> = 10<sup>16−25</sup> = 10<sup>−9</sup>, so p = 9: only about one ester molecule in 10<sup>9–10</sup> is an enolate at a time, enough for the reaction to proceed but too little to push it. The final deprotonation of acetoacetate, EAA + EtO<sup>−</sup> ⇌ enolate + EtOH, has K = 10<sup>16−11</sup> = 10<sup>5</sup>. Therefore K<sub>2</sub> = 10<sup>−3</sup> × 10<sup>5</sup> = 10<sup>2</sup>, log K<sub>2</sub> = 2: the condensation becomes favourable only because the product is consumed as its enolate, which is why stoichiometric base is required. ⚑ Real K for the neutral step is only approximately known; the value is a stated assumption.")),
 (69, ("(IChO-style) Acetophenone is heated with paraformaldehyde and dimethylamine hydrochloride (Mannich reaction) to give A. A is treated with MeI and then aqueous NaHCO<sub>3</sub> to give B (C<sub>9</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O) plus NMe<sub>3</sub>. B is then treated with diethyl malonate and catalytic NaOEt to give C. Give A, B and C and name the key mechanism converting A·MeI into B.",
   S([("A", "CN(C)CCC(=O)c1ccccc1"), ("B", "C=CC(=O)c1ccccc1"), ("C", "CCOC(=O)C(CCC(=O)c1ccccc1)C(=O)OCC")]),
   "A: PhCOCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NMe<sub>2</sub>; B: phenyl vinyl ketone; C: diethyl 2-(3-oxo-3-phenylpropyl)malonate; E1cB",
   "A is the Mannich base, formed by attack of the acetophenone enol on the iminium CH<sub>2</sub>=NMe<sub>2</sub><sup>+</sup>. Quaternisation turns NMe<sub>2</sub> into the good leaving group NMe<sub>3</sub>. Mild base removes the acidic α-H (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 25 in DMSO, accessible with bicarbonate in equilibrium) to give the enolate which expels NMe<sub>3</sub> in an E1cB step, delivering the very reactive enone B. B is a Michael acceptor: the malonate carbanion adds 1,4 and protonation gives C (1,5-dicarbonyl).")),
 (70, ("(Olympiad style) 2-Methylcyclohexanone is converted to its pyrrolidine enamine (cat. TsOH, toluene, Dean-Stark). The enamine is then treated with methyl acrylate and the mixture is hydrolysed with aqueous acetic acid. Draw the major enamine regioisomer, give the final keto-ester and explain the regiochemistry. Why does the sequence not give a double Michael addition?",
   S([("major enamine", "CC1CCCC=C1N1CCCC1"), ("product", "COC(=O)CCC1CCCC(C)C1=O")]),
   "less substituted enamine; methyl 3-(3-methyl-2-oxocyclohexyl)propanoate (alkylation at C-6)",
   "In the enamine from 2-methylcyclohexanone the more substituted C=C would force the N-pyrrolidine ring out of conjugation because of A<sup>1,3</sup>(allylic 1,3) strain between the N-CH<sub>2</sub> and the methyl group. The nitrogen therefore conjugates better with the less substituted C=C (towards C-6), and this is the nucleophile. Michael addition at C-6 and hydrolysis of the resulting iminium/enamine gives the 2,6-disubstituted ketone. The ketone is released only in the final hydrolysis, after the acceptor has been consumed, so enamine alkylation stops at monoalkylation (unlike enolate alkylation, there is no proton-transfer equilibration to a more reactive polyalkylating enolate).")),
 (71, ("(JEE Advanced, multi-correct) Ambident enolates (see figure) react at carbon or at oxygen depending on conditions. Which of the following changes will <b>increase</b> the proportion of O-alkylation of a ketone enolate? (A) Using a primary alkyl tosylate in place of the corresponding alkyl iodide; (B) replacing THF by THF containing 10% HMPA; (C) replacing Li<sup>+</sup> as counter-cation by K<sup>+</sup>; (D) replacing the aprotic solvent by tert-butanol.",
   S([("C-nucleophile form", "CC(=O)[CH2-]"), ("O-nucleophile form", "CC([O-])=C")]),
   "A, B and C",
   "O-alkylation is favoured by a hard electrophile with a leaving group that is hard and ionic (OTs, OSO<sub>2</sub>OMe, Cl rather than I) because the oxygen holds most of the negative charge (greatest charge control). Free, dissociated enolates (HMPA or DMSO solvation of the cation, large K<sup>+</sup>, R<sub>4</sub>N<sup>+</sup>) also react more at oxygen, while tight Li–O ion pairs shield oxygen. A protic solvent such as t-BuOH hydrogen-bonds to the oxygen, blocking it and favouring C-alkylation, so D decreases O-alkylation.")),
 (72, ("(IChO-style) Cyclohexanone is treated with ethyl chloroacetate and t-BuOK (A, C<sub>10</sub>H<sub>16</sub>O<sub>3</sub>). A is saponified with NaOH, acidified and gently heated to give B (C<sub>7</sub>H<sub>12</sub>O) with loss of CO<sub>2</sub>. Identify A and B and explain the mechanism of the final step, explaining why B is an aldehyde and not a ketone.",
   S([("A", "CCOC(=O)C1OC12CCCCC2"), ("B", "O=CC1CCCCC1")]),
   "A: ethyl 1-oxaspiro[2.5]octane-2-carboxylate (glycidic ester); B: cyclohexanecarbaldehyde",
   "Darzens condensation: the chloroacetate enolate adds to the ketone, and the alkoxide displaces Cl intramolecularly (3-exo-tet) to give the spiro-epoxy ester A. After hydrolysis the epoxy acid loses CO<sub>2</sub> with simultaneous opening of the strained epoxide: the electrons of the C–COOH bond form a C=C, and the C–O bond to the spiro carbon breaks, leaving oxygen on the former α-carbon as an enol(ate) of an aldehyde. Tautomerisation gives the aldehyde B (one-carbon homologation of the ketone to the aldehyde).")),
 (73, ("(Olympiad style) 2-Chlorocyclohexanone in which the carbon bearing Cl (C-2) is enriched with <sup>13</sup>C is treated with NaOMe/MeOH to give methyl cyclopentanecarboxylate. (a) Give the key intermediate. (b) Where does the <sup>13</sup>C label end up in the product, and what would a semibenzilic (non-scrambling) pathway predict instead?",
   S([("SM", "O=C1CCCCC1Cl"), ("product", "COC(=O)C1CCCC1"), ("intermediate", "O=C1C2CCCC12")]),
   "cyclopropanone; label found about 1:1 at ring C-1 (bearing CO<sub>2</sub>Me) and ring C-2; semibenzilic would give label only at C-1",
   "Methoxide removes the α′-H at C-6 (the only acidic position, since C-2 has Cl); the enolate displaces Cl intramolecularly to give the bicyclic cyclopropanone (C-2 and C-6 now joined). MeO<sup>−</sup> adds to the C=O, and the cyclopropane C1–C2 or C1–C6 bond cleaves with equal probability (the cyclopropanone is symmetrical) to give the more stable carbanion, which is protonated. Cleavage of C1–C6 leaves the label on the carbon bearing CO<sub>2</sub>Me, cleavage of C1–C2 leaves it on the neighbouring CH<sub>2</sub>. A semibenzilic mechanism (migration of C-6 to C-2 with chloride loss) would keep the label on the carbon bearing the ester. The observed ≈ 1:1 distribution supports the cyclopropanone. ⚑ This is for enolisable α′-H; α-halo ketones with no α′-H react by the semibenzilic pathway.")),
 (74, ("(Olympiad style) Salicylaldehyde reacts with diethyl malonate in ethanol with catalytic piperidine at room temperature to give a crystalline lactone (C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>4</sub>). Give its structure and the sequence of steps, and explain why a weak amine base is used rather than NaOEt.",
   None,
   "ethyl 2-oxo-2H-chromene-3-carboxylate (ethyl coumarin-3-carboxylate)",
   "Knoevenagel condensation: piperidine forms an iminium ion with the aldehyde (and/or buffers the malonate as its enolate), the malonate carbanion adds and dehydration (E1cB) gives the ortho-hydroxybenzylidene malonate. The Z-configured (ester cis to the ring OH) isomer is trapped by intramolecular transesterification of the phenol on one ester, losing EtOH, to give the coumarin (C<sub>12</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>4</sub>). A mild amine avoids Cannizzaro-type and ester-scrambling chemistry and a large equilibrium concentration of phenoxide, and since salicylaldehyde has no α-H the only enolisable partner is malonate (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 13).")),
 (75, ("(Olympiad style) (E)-Crotyl benzyl ether, PhCH<sub>2</sub>OCH<sub>2</sub>CH=CHCH<sub>3</sub>, is treated with n-BuLi in THF at −78 °C and allowed to warm to 0 °C, then quenched. (a) Give the major product and name the rearrangement. (b) A minor constitutional isomer forms increasingly at higher temperature; identify it and its mechanism. (c) How many stereoisomers does the major product have?",
   None,
   "[2,3]-Wittig: 2-methyl-1-phenylbut-3-en-1-ol; minor from [1,2]-Wittig; 4 stereoisomers",
   "n-BuLi removes the benzylic α-H (adjacent to O, activated by Ph) to give a carbanion. In a concerted five-membered envelope TS, the carbanion attacks the γ-carbon (CH–CH<sub>3</sub>) of the allyl unit while the O–CH<sub>2</sub> bond breaks, so the allyl group is transposed ([2,3]-sigmatropic shift) and the branched homoallylic alcohol results. The competing [1,2]-Wittig proceeds by homolysis to a radical pair that recombines at the original CH<sub>2</sub> to give 1-phenylpent-3-en-1-ol; this pathway becomes more competitive at higher temperature. The major product has two stereocentres (C-1 and C-2): 2<sup>2</sup> = 4 stereoisomers (2 diastereomers).")),
 (76, ("(Olympiad style) Two experiments are described. (a) Ph<sub>2</sub>C(OH)SiMe<sub>3</sub> with a catalytic amount of KOtBu in THF gives an isomer P. (b) PhCH<sub>2</sub>OSiMe<sub>3</sub> with sec-BuLi in THF at −78 °C gives, after aqueous work-up, an isomer Q. Identify P and Q, name both rearrangements, and explain why they run in opposite directions.",
   S([("P", "C[Si](C)(C)OC(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1"), ("Q", "OC([Si](C)(C)C)c1ccccc1")]),
   "P = Ph<sub>2</sub>CH–OSiMe<sub>3</sub> ([1,2]-Brook); Q = PhCH(OH)SiMe<sub>3</sub> ([1,2]-retro-Brook)",
   "(a) The alkoxide attacks silicon to form a three-membered pentacoordinate silicate (Si–O bond formation is very favourable, ≈ 110 kcal/mol vs ≈ 75 for Si–C), cleaving the C–Si bond to give the diphenylmethyl carbanion, which is protonated by more substrate (chain reaction). With K<sup>+</sup> the O–metal bond is weak, so the O-to-C silyl shift is downhill because the diphenyl-stabilised carbanion is accessible. (b) With Li, deprotonation of the benzylic C–H gives an α-silyloxy organolithium; the very strong Li–O bond makes the lithium alkoxide the thermodynamic sink, so the silyl group migrates C-ward (retro-Brook). Silicon configuration is retained in both. ⚑ The direction is governed by counter-cation, solvent and carbanion-stabilising groups, not by the rearrangement name alone.")),
 (77, ("(Olympiad style) 1-Chloro-2,2,2-triphenylethane (Ph<sub>3</sub>CCH<sub>2</sub>Cl) is treated with excess lithium (or sodium) in THF; the organometallic is then quenched with water. A hydrocarbon C<sub>20</sub>H<sub>18</sub> is obtained that is <b>not</b> 1,1,1-triphenylethane. (a) Identify it. (b) Give the intermediate. (c) Would PhC(CH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>Li (neophyllithium) undergo the analogous shift?",
   S([("SM", "ClCC(c1ccccc1)(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1"), ("product", "C(c1ccccc1)C(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1")]),
   "1,1,2-triphenylethane (via Ph<sub>2</sub>C(Li)CH<sub>2</sub>Ph); neophyllithium does not",
   "Halogen–metal exchange gives Ph<sub>3</sub>C–CH<sub>2</sub>M, a primary alkyl anion (conjugate acid pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 50). A phenyl migrates 1,2 (Grovenstein–Zimmerman rearrangement): the carbanion adds to the ipso carbon of an adjacent phenyl, forming a spiro[2.5]octadienyl anion (a cyclohexadienyl anion fused to a cyclopropane), and the three-membered ring opens at the other C–C bond to put the charge on CPh<sub>2</sub>. The product anion Ph<sub>2</sub>C(−)CH<sub>2</sub>Ph is a diphenylmethyl-type carbanion (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 32 for Ph<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>), strongly stabilised; water gives Ph<sub>2</sub>CHCH<sub>2</sub>Ph. For neophyllithium the shift would give a tertiary alkyl anion, less stable than the primary one, so it does not rearrange (unlike the neophyl radical). ⚑ Whether the spiro species is a true intermediate or a transition state is debated.")),
 (78, ("(IChO-style) 2-Methylcyclohexane-1,3-dione reacts with methyl vinyl ketone (MVK) in the presence of a base (Et<sub>3</sub>N or pyrrolidine), then the intermediate is heated. Give (i) the Michael adduct, (ii) the cyclic β-ketol, and (iii) the final enone (Wieland–Miescher ketone). State the number of new C–C bonds formed and the number of stereogenic centres in the final product.",
   S([("Michael adduct", "CC1(CCC(C)=O)C(=O)CCCC1=O"), ("ketol", "CC12CCC(=O)CC1(O)CCCC2=O"), ("enone", "CC12CCC(=O)C=C1CCCC2=O")]),
   "2 new C–C bonds; 1 stereocentre (quaternary C-CH<sub>3</sub>); product racemic with achiral base",
   "The dione (pK<sub>a</sub> ≈ 10, enolised readily) is C-2 alkylated by Michael addition to MVK to give the triketone (first C–C bond). The CH<sub>3</sub> of the side-chain methyl ketone then forms an enol(ate) and adds to one of the two enantiotopic ring C=O groups (intramolecular aldol, second C–C bond, six-membered ring) to give the bicyclic β-ketol. Dehydration (E1cB) of the ketol gives the conjugated enone. The only stereocentre is the quaternary ring-junction carbon bearing CH<sub>3</sub>; an achiral base gives the racemate, while chiral amine catalysts (e.g. (S)-proline) desymmetrise the two C=O groups by enamine formation and give an enantioenriched product.")),
 (79, ("(IChO-style) Compound X (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>2</sub>) gives a negative Tollens test and a positive iodoform test (the other product being succinic acid). On heating with dilute aqueous NaOH, X forms Y (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O). Identify X and Y, give the mechanism, and say why a cyclopropane- or cyclobutane-forming aldol is not observed. What would heptane-2,6-dione give under the same conditions?",
   None,
   "X = hexane-2,5-dione; Y = 3-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-one; heptane-2,6-dione gives 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one",
   "X has two methyl ketones (iodoform positive), no CHO, and C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>10</sub>O<sub>2</sub> fits hexane-2,5-dione. The CH<sub>3</sub> enolate (C-1) attacks the C-5 carbonyl in an intramolecular aldol (5-exo-trig, five-membered ring), and E1cB dehydration gives the conjugated cyclopentenone Y (C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>8</sub>O). The alternative enolate at C-3 would have to attack C-5 and form a three-membered ring (and C-3 onto C-2 gives nothing), so it is strained and reversible. Heptane-2,6-dione similarly closes a six-membered ring and gives 3-methylcyclohex-2-enone. ⚑ The iodoform detail is only a diagnostic for CH<sub>3</sub>CO– groups.")),
 (80, ("(Olympiad style) Allyl propanoate is treated with LDA in THF at −78 °C, then Me<sub>3</sub>SiCl, and the mixture is warmed to room temperature and hydrolysed. (a) Give the product and the intermediate that undergoes the key step. (b) What do (E)-crotyl propanoate and a 'THF/HMPA' variant of the same procedure give, and why is the diastereoselectivity dependent on solvent?",
   None,
   "2-methylpent-4-enoic acid (via silyl ketene acetal, Ireland–Claisen [3,3]); crotyl gives 2,3-dimethylpent-4-enoic acid (two diastereomers selectively)",
   "LDA forms the lithium ester enolate, which is trapped by TMSCl at oxygen as the allyl silyl ketene acetal CH<sub>3</sub>CH=C(OTMS)OCH<sub>2</sub>CH=CH<sub>2</sub>. This undergoes a [3,3]-sigmatropic (Claisen) shift via a chair-like TS at room temperature: the new C–C bond forms between the enolate carbon and the terminal allyl carbon, and the C–O allyl bond breaks. Hydrolysis of the silyl ester gives the acid. With (E)-crotyl the product has two stereocentres; THF favours one enolate geometry and THF/HMPA the other, and the geometry, together with the chair TS, delivers opposite diastereomers. ⚑ The labels E/Z for the enolate flip depending on CIP priorities (OLi vs OSiMe<sub>3</sub>), so state geometry by drawing.")),
 (81, ("(Olympiad style) 2-Methylcyclohexanone tosylhydrazone is treated with 2.2 equiv of n-BuLi in THF (0 °C to rt). (a) What is the reactive intermediate and which alkene is formed after aqueous quench? (b) If DMF is added before work-up, what is formed? (c) How does the regiochemistry differ from Bamford–Stevens decomposition in a protic medium?",
   S([("hydrazone", "CC1CCCCC1=NNS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(C)cc1"), ("after H2O", "CC1C=CCCC1"), ("after DMF", "O=CC1=CCCCC1C")]),
   "vinyllithium; 3-methylcyclohexene; 6-methylcyclohex-1-ene-1-carbaldehyde",
   "The first equivalent of BuLi removes N–H and the second removes an α-H syn to the tosylamide anion, on the less hindered side (C-6; the NTs sits anti to the CH(CH<sub>3</sub>) side), giving a dianion. This loses Ts<sup>−</sup> to give a diazenide, which loses N<sub>2</sub> to give the vinyllithium at C-1, with the C=C between C-1 and C-6 (the less substituted alkene). Quenching with H<sub>2</sub>O gives 3-methylcyclohexene; DMF acylates the vinyllithium to give the α,β-unsaturated aldehyde after hydrolysis. Bamford–Stevens decomposition in protic media proceeds through carbocations and tends to give the more substituted (Zaitsev-like) alkene, e.g. 1-methylcyclohexene as the main component.")),
]
